<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Beatitudes: The Path to True Happiness" cic="CCC 1716-1729" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook. Take your time. The beatitudes are not a race; they're a journey.</p>

<h3>1. Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
  <li>What did St. Augustine say about the human heart's desire for happiness, and why have his words echoed for sixteen centuries?</li>
  <li>What does the Greek word <em>makarios</em> (blessed) mean, and how is it different from our everyday idea of &laquo;happiness&raquo;?</li>
  <li>List all eight beatitudes in order, with the promise attached to each one.</li>
  <li>Why does the Catechism call the beatitudes a &laquo;portrait of Christ&raquo;? What does that mean?</li>
  <li>What is &laquo;eternal beatitude&raquo; and what does the Catechism teach about the &laquo;beatific vision&raquo;?</li>
  <li>Who was Blessed Chiara Badano, and how did she live the beatitudes in her suffering?</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>2. The Great Paradox: World vs. Jesus</h3>

<p>Fill in the right-hand column with what Jesus says in the corresponding beatitude:</p>

